.TH REFER 1
.SH NAME
refer \- cite works by their keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B refer
[
.BI -p " bib"
] [
.BI -S " style"
] [
.B -e
] [
.B -m
] [
.B -i
] [
.BI -o " xy"
] [
.BI -a " xy"
] [
.B -sa
] [
.BI -r " xy,..."
] [
.B -C
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Refer
is a preprocessor for
.IR troff (1):
it reads troff's input from the files named, or its standard input
.RB ( -
names it too), and writes it with each
citation set from a database of works, refer's own or BibTeX's
(a file ending in
.BR .bib ).
A citation is a work's key and, after it, the place in it:
.IP
.EX
\&.cite smith-swing 315-16
\&.cite grazer 12; thoreau 177-178
.EE
.PP
or, within a line,
.BR "\e*[cite smith-swing 315-16]" ;
.B .bibliography
sets the works cited.
Without a style it is Ali Gholami Rudi's Neatrefer, of which it is a
translation, and writes what Neatrefer writes.
.PP
A work may be written out where it is cited, in refer's fields, instead
of being looked up by its key:
.IP
.EX
\&.[ [
%A J. F. Ossanna
%T Nroff/Troff User's Manual
%D 1976
\&.]]
.EE
.PP
With
.B "-S chicago"
it sets citations as
.I "The Chicago Manual of Style"
(18th edition) sets them, in notes and a bibliography: a work in full
the first time it is cited in a chapter, shortened after; a book, a
chapter of one, an article, a thesis, a classical work; et al.,
translators and editors; inclusive pages (315\(en16); the bibliography
sorted as the Manual sorts it: by the first author, a work by that
author alone before those written with others, those by the others'
names, then by title.
.IR Book (1)
runs it so.
.PP
With
.B "-S numeric"
it sets them as journals of the sciences and engineering do (IEEE's,
ACM's): in the line, as
.B [1]
or, at a place in the work,
.BR "[1, p.\ 5]" ,
before the punctuation the line ends with; numbered in the order the
works are first cited, a work cited again by its own number; and the
references in that order, each hung from its number, its authors by
their initials.
.TP
.BI -p " bib"
The database.
.TP
.BI -S " style"
Set citations in the style
.RB ( chicago
or
.BR numeric ),
its options after its name, each after a comma
.RB ( chicago,british,nodoi,nourl ):
.B british
titles in single quotation marks, quotations within them double, the
punctuation after the marks;
.B nodoi
and
.B nourl
neither DOI nor URL (both styles); an option the style does not know is refused.
.TP
.BI -r " xy,..."
The requests that begin a chapter, for a style
.RB ( chapter ).
.TP
.B -e
Accumulate the references, for a list at the end.
.TP
.B -m
Merge the references of one
.B .[
.B .]
block.
.TP
.B -i
Authors' first and middle names as initials.
.TP
.BI -o " xy"
.TP
.BI -a " xy"
The citation's macro, and the author and year citation's
.RB ( \e*[ xy " label" ] ).
.TP
.B -sa
Sort by the authors' last names.
.TP
.B -C
Set a
.B .[
.B .]
citation as Bell Laboratories' refer did, for documents written for
it (Kernighan's manual of
.IR pic (1),
say): joined to the end of the line before it, between what follows
.B .[
and what follows
.B .]
on their lines
.RB ( ".[ [" " and " .]] " give " [1] ),
or, with neither, between
.B \e*([.
and
.B \e*(.]
(-ms's superscript); a line that ends in a full stop or a comma has
it each side of the citation, in
.B \e*(<.
and
.B \e*(>.
(-ms keeps the one its style wants).
Neatrefer sets the citation on a line of its own.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/refer
.SH EXAMPLES
Kernighan's manual of pic, as it was made (its macros ask for the
Linotron 202's font for programs, PO, unless
.B pf
names another):
.IP
.EX
refer -C -e pic.man | pic | eqn -C | troff -ms '-dpf=CR' |
	tr2pdf >pic.pdf
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR book (1),
.IR troff (1)
